1
00:00:00,650 --> 00:00:03,719
Two paper structures rise from a bright
tabletop.

2
00:00:03,719 --> 00:00:08,321
Both become taller, but one accelerates
upward while the other begins to

3
00:00:08,321 --> 00:00:09,856
flatten as it grows.

4
00:00:15,650 --> 00:00:18,286
The first structure follows an exponential
model.

5
00:00:18,286 --> 00:00:22,806
A larger current amount produces a larger
instantaneous increase, so its steps

6
00:00:22,806 --> 00:00:24,689
become taller along the path.

7
00:00:30,650 --> 00:00:33,311
The second structure follows a logarithmic
curve.

8
00:00:33,311 --> 00:00:39,012
Its level keeps rising, but equal input
steps add less and less height as the

9
00:00:39,012 --> 00:00:39,772
input increases.

10
00:00:45,650 --> 00:00:48,554
Small tangent ribbons touch the two
curves.

11
00:00:48,554 --> 00:00:53,532
Their directions reveal local rates,
giving a precise way to compare changes

12
00:00:53,532 --> 00:00:55,606
that the eye only notices.

13
00:01:00,650 --> 00:01:03,359
A logarithm door admits only positive
inputs.

14
00:01:03,359 --> 00:01:06,069
An exponential door accepts any real
input.

15
00:01:06,069 --> 00:01:09,939
The derivative rule must respect the
function behind that door.

16
00:01:15,650 --> 00:01:20,610
Choose a point on each curve. Why is the
next small increase different?

17
00:01:20,610 --> 00:01:25,189
Find their derivative rules and explain
the rate at your chosen input.
